But wait: the JS side currently uses `tempFile` for pr_comments/issue_comments/threads/comment. If I change to a per-run mktemp -d dir, the `--body-file` operand becomes `<uniqdir>/comment.md` — canon normalizes `[--body-file] [*]` → `[BODY_FILE]`. Fine.

Now — will the parity harness still pass with the JS calling `mktemp -d` through exec? The exec stub in the harness is `cp.execSync(r.command, {env: sc.env, ...})`. The command would be `mktemp -d` — real mktemp exists in PATH (/usr/bin). It creates a dir in /tmp. Fine. But careful: the JS temp-dir creation must go through `io.exec` (the transport), because in production there's no fs access (GraalJS: no fs module). So the entry needs to:
